10 REM THE DOUBLE DIVIDE TAKES ITS QUOTIENT AS THREE DIGITS (16, 24 and 24 bits; ddigit, p91),
20 REM each a host estimate set right by the exact remainder, where the ROM takes 64 bits one
30 REM at a time (0DE5H).  The 64 bits are the same.  These twelve quotients are the ones an
40 REM estimate gets wrong: one too high (the next digit is all ones) or one too low (it is
50 REM all zeros), at the first, second and third digit, two of each.  A# and B# are POKEd
60 REM as their 8 stored bytes, so the operands are exact; the 8 bytes of A#/B# follow them.
70 CLEAR 100:F=0:I=0:J=0:N=0:X=0:VA=0:VB=0:VC=0:A#=1:B#=1:C#=0:ON ERROR GOTO 900
80 VA=VARPTR(A#):VB=VARPTR(B#):VC=VARPTR(C#)
90 READ N:FOR J=1 TO N
100 FOR I=0 TO 7:READ X:POKE VA+I,X:NEXT:FOR I=0 TO 7:READ X:POKE VB+I,X:NEXT
110 C#=A#/B#:FOR I=0 TO 7:READ X:IF PEEK(VC+I)<>X THEN PRINT "FAIL: case";J;"byte";I;"is";PEEK(VC+I);"not";X:F=1
120 NEXT:NEXT
130 IF STR$(1#/3#)<>" .3333333333333333" OR STR$(-7#/2#)<>"-3.5" OR STR$(1D10/1D-10)<>" 1D+20" THEN PRINT "FAIL: plain quotients:";1#/3#;-7#/2#;1D10/1D-10:F=1
